;  9 (foot); 2.7 (metre);  29 (pounds)", "marker": "NOT_ADMISSIBLE", "harvest": "NEEDS_JUDGE"}, "traits": {"outcome": "ABSTAIN_NOT_ADMITTED", "answer_or_proposed": "not run", "marker": "NOT_ADMISSIBLE", "harvest": "NEEDS_JUDGE", "reason": "pending slow clock", "provisional": true}, "topic_node": {"outcome": "ANSWER_PROVISIONAL", "answer_or_proposed": "medtop:08000000", "marker": "BELOW_BAR", "harvest": "DISCARD"}}, "total_ms": 384.63, "over_budget": false, "seq": 22, "read_at": 1795289835.648582, "lag_s": 145341.07, "gaps": ["WINDOW_CUT", "TOPIC_DISAGREE", "LANDING_SPLIT", "RERANK_DISAGREE", "UNPLACED_HIGH", "NOT_ATTACHED"]}, "text": "‘Forever our Fiona’: Cincinnati Zoo’s famous hippo dies after swallowing visitor’s hat. Fiona, the hippopotamus whose premature birth at the Cincinnati Zoo in 2017 made her a global sensation, has died aged nine after swallowing a visitor’s knit hat that accidentally landed in her enclosure. The zoo said Thursday that she died despite repeated attempts by veterinarians and keepers to treat the resulting medical emergency. “It is with extreme sadness that we share with you the devastating news that Fiona did not make it after multiple attempts from our amazing vets and keeper team to help her through what ended up being a medical emergency,” the zoo said in a statement. Fiona swallowed the hat about 11 days ago after a visitor tossed it into the air. A ceiling fan caught the object and deflected it through a small opening above the enclosure’s viewing windows, zoo spokesperson Michelle Curley said. Curley described the incident as an accident. The zoo had been providing Fiona with round-the-clock care, keeping her hydrated, offering treats and monitoring her condition. Specialists advised that surgery was not an option for a full-grown hippo, the zoo said. The veterinary team also borrowed a vibration plate from a horse farm, hoping that the movement would help the hat pass through Fiona’s digestive tract. She was encouraged to stand on it several times a day. “Despite multiple attempts to move the hat out of her system, it was ultimately too much to overcome,” the zoo said. Mike Wenninger, the zoo’s director of animal health, said veterinarians had sedated Fiona twice to assess her condition. An ultrasound on Tuesday showed some bunching of her intestines. A colonoscopy using a 9-foot (2.7-metre) scope did not locate the hat, he said. Fiona’s death prompted an immediate outpouring of grief. Visitors began leaving flowers and cards beside a hippo statue outside the zoo within an hour of the announcement. One note read, “Forever our Fiona.” Accorfing to news agency AP, Cincinnati mayor Aftab Pureval called Fiona “a symbol of perseverance and joy”, while Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine described her as “a true icon”. The Cincinnati Bengals also paid tribute, calling her “the queen of the Queen City”. Fiona was born six weeks premature, weighing just 29 pounds (13.2 kilograms), and initially struggled to stand and nurse. Her recovery was documented by the zoo, drawing a worldwide online following and bringing renewed attention to the facility after the 2016 killing of gorilla Harambe.", "stages_full": {"windows": {"windows": 12, "cap": 12, "pct_body_read": 94, "offsets_text": [[88, 292], [293, 516], [517, 719], [720, 917], [918, 1135], [1136, 1325], [1325, 1507], [1507, 1684], [1685, 1830], [1831, 2022], [2022, 2195], [2196, 2383]]}, "scan_dates": {"spots": {"attribution": 7, "date": 2, "entity": 26, "event": 2, "modal": 1, "money": 1, "negation": 3, "quantity": 2, "quote": 5}, "dates": [["Thursday", "2026-11-19"], ["Tuesday", "2026-11-17"]], "dates_resolved": 2, "anchor": "2026-11-20", "time_proxy": {"label": "time_proxy", "marker": "PROVISIONAL", "offset_days": [-1, -3], "n": 2, "of": "resolved date minus published date, in days"}}, "facts": {"kinds": {"amount": 7, "date": 2, "name": 26, "triple": 47}, "stated": 43, "unbound": 39, "invalid": 0, "stated_amount": 7, "stated_date": 2, "quarantined": 0, "held": 